course: dictionary
lesson: 10987
title: Warren: a rabbit's home or a maze of streets
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one you need. «Wild rabbits live in groups in a network of burrows called a warren.» Here the sentence is about rabbits and burrows, so a warren must be a home for rabbits under the ground.
explain: Now look at «a warren of little streets». Streets are not animals, so the clue is the place. A warren can also be a building or area with many narrow, confusing passages. Ask yourself: is this sentence about animals, or about places and buildings?
explain: Warren is also a name, like a boy's first name. Look for clues such as a capital letter, or a person doing something. When you can swap in a meaning and the sentence still makes sense, you have probably found the right one.
fact: A warren is a group of connected underground holes and tunnels where rabbits live.
fact: «Rabbits live in burrows called a warren and warn each other of danger by thumping the ground with their back feet.»
fact: «Some warrens have been used for over a hundred years.»
fact: A warren can also be a building or area with many narrow, confusing passages, like «a warren of little streets».
fact: Warren is also a boy's first name and a family name, so a capital W can mean a person.
exercise: «Rabbits live in burrows called a warren and warn each other of danger by thumping the ground.» What is a warren in this sentence? | A type of shoe | A loud drum | A tall tree where birds nest | *A group of connected underground holes and tunnels where rabbits live
exercise: «The old town was a warren of little streets.» What does warren mean here? | A wide, open field with no paths | *A place with many narrow, confusing passages | A rabbit's sleeping box | A kind of boat
exercise: «Warren and his sister walked to school.» What does Warren mean here? | A type of tunnel | A rabbit's underground home | A busy street | *A boy's first name
test: Which meaning fits «The rabbits thump the ground to warn the warren of danger»? | *Underground tunnels where rabbits live | A narrow street in an old town | A name for a dog | A busy shopping centre
test: In «Warren's bike has a flat tyre», what tells you Warren is a name? | The word tyre | A rabbit is mentioned | The word bike | *The capital W, and the 's showing the bike belongs to someone
exam: Read «The warren was dark and full of tunnels, so the rabbits knew every turn.» What does warren mean here? | A kind of hat | *A group of underground tunnels where rabbits live | A shop selling toys | A sunny field of grass
